into another room and and do the top down perspective part of the level, when you go back, the ship

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.560
is like right where it was.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:25.820
And I just think there's something really neat about that, that it didn't just like, you

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:30.280
know, like load up a spawn point next to the door or something, even though that's not where

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.900
you left it. It felt like the world was alive in that respect.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:37.320
Right. That even extends to like enemies you kill.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.120
Exactly. There's persistence that you just don't.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:45.580
I did not expect to see because every game we've played, there's no such thing as persistence.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:52.240
Like you if you go off screen a little bit and come back, that enemy you just killed is

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:53.660
going to be magically there again.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:59.140
But between the car being still there and the enemy still being dead like that, that's the

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.820
thing so simple that it just like was amazing to me.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:07.720
And the thing I was talking about earlier in respect to Samus from Metroid is just what

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:12.540
did you guys think though, as you're, as you're playing mostly as the tank, what was that like

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:16.440
in terms of exploration and just like using that as your main character?

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:18.240
Like does, does that feel weird?

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:22.160
Or I know Sean, you really liked the mechanics of the car itself.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:25.080
So did it just feel weird to be a car as your character?

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:30.000
I think that once you kind of got out of your head about the fact that you're a car and usually,

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:34.180
you know, in a game like this, you're a person, it didn't bother me at all.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:37.340
Like, you know, it didn't feel like usually in a game like this, if you were to get into

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:41.640
a car, it would be like to get to be faster and to be a little more powerful, but then you

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.820
can't fit through tight spaces and things like that.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.580
But the whole, the levels are designed to kind of like fit the tank.

00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:50.640
And it really is kind of designed basically like it would be if you, if you weren't in

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:57.540
the vehicle. And then it just gives you options or sometimes the necessity to get out, which I thought,

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.320
you know, it was pretty welcome because there was no, it first of all felt like it gave context

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:06.160
to all the power ups and, you know, you might, you get missiles and you get other things and

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:09.540
it just didn't feel like it's just like, oh, this is all stuff on your, you know, in your

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.940
hands. It's like, no, this, you're actually upgrading this machine.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:18.580
Yeah, Joe, I think for sure, once you get, you know, you mentioned missiles, which are

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:22.400
clear things that like a tank would have that necessarily like a person wouldn't have, although

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:23.480
Samus does get missiles.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:28.580
I think it's once you get those, like the dive engine and the wall upgrades that you're

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:33.440
able to like take the traction of the wheels and go like climbing up vertically on the walls.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:35.820
And I forget what the second one lets you do it.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:40.320
I think you can hang on the ceiling or something that like gives context to the tank.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:44.220
So it really is those power ups that help a lot more to sell the idea of why they chose

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:45.820
to have you control the tank.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:50.140
Granted, you know, because the Japanese story is so different, it just makes sense to be

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:52.600
in a tank, I suppose, instead of when you're trying to save a frog.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:58.180
It's basically the opposite reaction that I had to the movie Cars, because when I saw Cars,

00:19:58.340 --> 00:19:59.720
I'm like, this doesn't make sense.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:05.260
I can't accept that these are cars talking and doing things and, you know, constructing buildings

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.240
and all, like I could not accept that.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:13.480
But all you needed to do was give me the context of a, of a person getting into and out of

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:17.380
said car. And I'm on, I'm on your side.

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:21.540
There should be a special cut of the movie Cars, or at the very beginning, a guy just

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:22.380
gets into the car.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:25.240
And then you know that the whole movie is controlling the car.

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.720
Yes. That would make everything feel much better for me.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:33.200
I do want to mention one thing that we, that we didn't talk about with the, with the getting

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:37.560
in and out of the tank is that as much as I think it was a cool mechanic, I think it

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.600
was maybe a little underused.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:44.340
I didn't, I, there were maybe one or two times where I was shooting over something and I could

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:48.020
get out of the car and like kind of go prone and shoot something that's low to the ground.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.560
And otherwise there were a couple of times where it makes you get out.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:55.700
But I think for the most part, the fact that you can get stuck when you get out of the

00:20:55.700 --> 00:21:00.140
car, like the first time I got out of the car was where, where it's, I keep calling it

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:04.200
a car. The tank is where it's prompting you to, like we were talking about before, when you

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:05.060
have to go underwater.

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:09.900
But I got out of the tank and jumped from the top platform into the water because I wasn't

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:13.200
sure I'd be able to get the tank back up there thinking, oh, well, there's a ladder

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.020
on the other side. I'm sure there's some way to get back around.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:21.420
But to my knowledge, there's no way to get back up to where I left the tank from there.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:24.620
I was just stuck as the person trying to jump up there.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:29.140
And the other problem with being a person is there's an absurd, well, realistically, it's

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:34.360
not absurd, but for a video game, an absurd level of fall damage where even just jumping

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:39.360
a little higher, like landing on a platform a little lower than what you jumped on will

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:40.580
automatically kill you.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.520
So I found myself completely stuck.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:48.480
Yeah. And I had to just die in order to get back to my tank and lose one of one of your lives,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:49.380
which are not infinite.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:54.300
Yeah. I never was separated from my car like that.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.400
I also didn't have this.

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.280
Like, I guess the aha moment for me happened somewhere else because I don't remember a

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:07.660
moment in the water that made that I felt like a prompt that like, oh, you got to get

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:10.480
out of your car now. It was somewhere entirely different.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:16.600
Like I was able to just maneuver in the water fine with the car or the truck, the tank.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:19.740
Make up your mind.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:28.520
But I guess I just never felt like I like the mechanic, but I guess I just used it even

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:33.260
less than you guys did because I if it was too low to the ground, I just sort of jumped

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:40.280
over them. So I only got out of the car when I had to go into a into one of the sub sub rooms.

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:44.460
Yeah, I think what you did is honestly probably the smarter way to deal with that anyways.

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:47.900
I kind of just more was like testing it like, well, if I can't hit him now, could I hit him

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:52.160
as a person? But I don't think there's ever like a reason why I would like, well, I need to kill that

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:53.540
guy rather than just go around him.

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:59.080
Yeah. Which is just another reason why like as cool as as cool as that mechanic is, they didn't

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:00.680
put enough reason for me to use it.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:06.780
It's definitely missing a puzzle mechanic to the world in the sense of like reasons to

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:11.680
explore and leave your ship that like you have to then like later after solving the puzzle,

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:14.580
you're able to like retrieve your ship from like a ship, whatever.

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:15.900
We're calling this thing all sorts of things.

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:18.020
Yeah. This is why they want you to call it Sophia.

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:21.020
It would be so much easier if we all just called it Sophia.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:25.800
Okay, Sophia. You know, but if you have to do it that way, that would be like a good excuse to get out.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:27.800
I actually used it for a different reason.

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:34.920
And that's because when you are the main character, Jason, you have a separate health bar.

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:39.040
And once you get back into Sophia, you gain all your health again.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:45.080
So it is kind of a cheese mechanic there, if you will, that you can get out, fight as

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:48.280
a full health character and then get back in the car.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:51.900
And this is assuming you're low on health or whatever in the tank for a reason to get

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:56.560
out of it. But I thought that was neat that they're able to keep track of first off, like so many

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:00.100
different things, the position of your tank, the health of your tank, the health of you,

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:06.240
the enemies you've killed, but also that, you know, there's like a reason to be out

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:12.880
of the car for lack of any other reasons, like there's no puzzles or progression moments

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:16.360
outside of the fact that, you know, and we haven't talked about this yet and we probably

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:21.140
should, that there are doors you have to go through to get to these top-down perspectives

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:23.880
to make progress through the different areas.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:24.860
Do you guys want to talk about that?

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:37.260
Yeah. So usually there's a boss or some just power-ups will be hidden behind these doors that you

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:41.320
can only go into if you are in pedestrian mode.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:49.740
And then you get, you know, like that sort of zoomed in Zelda shooter experience.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:54.520
Um, and, uh, I mean, that, that's pretty much it.

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:56.920
Like you, you go in there for power-ups, you go in there for bosses.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:02.440
Yeah. And it has like this interesting mechanic though, too, of two different forms of attack.

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:08.480
Uh, and, and I really couldn't, and maybe this was just like my failure to understand,

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:14.580
but I was mostly just using my regular attack because it seems like the special attack has

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:18.140
like a certain, it has to hit a certain sweet spot for it to register as a hit.

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:18.980
Is it a grenade?

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:20.240
Is that why it's like?

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:21.180
I think it's a grenade. Yeah.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:26.980
Got it. Okay. It's just going to be damage at the end of that, like shoot tail thing.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.860
It's not going to be like a, like a, the equivalent to like a hit scan.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:33.340
Right. Go ahead.

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:38.700
Well, the only time I found myself using that grenade or whatever it was, was when there's

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:42.520
sometimes when you're, when your gun is powered up, it's not powered up at all.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:46.340
The game pays a little too much attention to the fact that your character is right-handed.

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:47.500
Have you guys had this problem?

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:49.460
Yes. Yeah. Like, I get that.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:52.620
It's like, it wouldn't be realistic if the bullet came out from like the center of him

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:57.120
or anything, but like there were times where I had to be up against a wall shooting like

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:00.960
along the wall and something else up against the wall wouldn't get hit because like my

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:04.100
right hand was like too far out and you couldn't like aim.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.760
So there was just no way of hitting it unless you went around it, went on the other side.

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:09.200
So your right arm would be up against the wall.

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:12.460
That's one of the most noticeable quirks for me of this mode.

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:17.540
Uh, I, I had this, I had to like trick some enemies into going in a certain direction so

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:18.760
that I could shoot them safely.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:20.820
Yeah. It was fun. Yeah.

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:21.940
Let, let me ask you this.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.780
Does it feel unnecessary to have this mode?

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:25.900
I don't think so at all.

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:30.540
I think that this, this mode kind of, to me, feels really important to the game because

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:35.640
it gives like that much more, I don't want to say meaning, but like it just gives that

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:38.680
much more reason for you to be doing all the exploring you're doing in the ship because

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:41.540
you're looking for these moments where you can get out and go through this.

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:46.100
Like, it's almost like, it's almost like the, the legend of Zelda has dungeons, but

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:49.840
then getting to the next dungeon is just like a different kind of dungeon instead of an

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:51.020
overworld. You know what I mean?

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:55.260
So to me, I think, I think it was really cool to like get to this point and be like, all

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:58.800
right, now I get to get out and like kind of do my like on foot exploring through this

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.940
sort of maze.

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:05.800
I mean, granted, they're probably less confusing than the, than the overworld, but it was just

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:10.980
a cool change of pace and it never felt with like the three kind of ways to play the game.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:16.160
It never felt like they were like, uh, in like contrast to each other.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:20.960
They always felt like they fit together so well, even like the, the top down levels would

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:26.420
have like themes and elements and music that fit with the, the section you were in on the

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:27.820
side scrolling tank levels.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:35.440
Yeah. I think it enriches, it enriches both modes, uh, because like whether, whether you enjoy

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:42.160
playing as Sophia, um, or not like the fact that there is something to, to break it up

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.920
a bit in these sub rooms slash boss battles.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:52.320
Uh, I think it, uh, it doesn't allow you to get as bored as you would otherwise.

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:54.440
Yeah, it definitely breaks the game up.

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:57.740
As you said, Sean, I just, you know, and I, I'm not critiquing it.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:00.260
I'm asking the question of if it needed to exist.

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:03.300
I don't, it's almost another problem with, with the game.

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:08.960
And maybe this is just itself in the, in the Metroidvania world of things that there usually

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:16.220
are some sorts of puzzles or, uh, reasons for exploring outside of areas that you normally

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:20.700
would. And in this one, it's kind of the same thing where when you go through one of these doors

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:24.600
and you enter this new thing, you're really just trying to get to the end of the area.

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:31.760
Uh, there's no like secret rooms or, um, or puzzles you have to solve or like move this

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:33.920
rock or, you know, and sometimes that can get annoying.

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.800
So move this rock is not something I'm a fan of.

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:42.320
It's just an example. Uh, sometimes it's getting annoying, but I think this game is just missing a, a puzzle

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.720
or strategy element to it.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:49.320
And it's just more of a, a action run and gun game at the end of the day.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:53.460
Yeah. I mean, I agree that it, you know, it would never hurt to add a puzzle element to it,

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:57.800
but I think that it's just does so well at just being the action run and gun game that

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:58.760
I I'm okay with it.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.820
Like the way it is.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:04.340
I think that there is something really cool about, uh, you know, going through one of these

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:10.160
like top down dungeons, if you will, getting, defeating the boss, getting out and being

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:13.180
like, all right, let's get the heck out of here and like get back in your, in your vehicle.

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:16.760
And like, I don't know, there's something very like action movie bad-ass about it.

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:18.440
That's like a little wish fulfillment.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:23.620
I agree. I think when it comes to games like this, where, where there's like upgrades that you

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:29.960
have to get, um, just the whole Metroidvania thing when it's done poorly, like the upgrades

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:34.700
just feel like keys, like the, like, Oh, you got this key and I can open that door.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:42.440
But in a game like this, where there is some creativity to the design of the, uh, uh, of the

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:49.000
upgrades you're getting and there's enough variety in level design and enemy placement

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:55.340
that they're not just keys to move forward, but they're also allowing you to have more

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:56.340
expressive play.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:02.760
Like you don't need to use all the, you don't need to use hover when you're fighting some

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:08.880
enemies, but it like, it's, it could be, it could be helpful in the way that you're playing.

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:12.520
Um, whereas somebody might stay on the ground, like when it's something, when it, when it

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:18.580
adds, uh, when it adds decisions to how you could just play the game normally, I think

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:19.600
that they've done that well.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:25.720
I would just like to see a, a giant map of Blaster Master with all five areas and see what

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:32.160
that looks like as a, you know, just a beginning to end of the game, getting only the necessary,

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:35.000
um, power-ups you need and stuff like that.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:38.600
And then also having a map of Metroid side by side with that.

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:43.960
And I just wonder how much more complex the Metroid one would be in a meaningful way, not

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:48.120
just complex for the sake of being difficult in a meaningful way, because a lot of this

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:52.300
game in, in a different sections of the areas as, as they're broken up between the rooms,

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:59.620
a lot of it is just kind of, you know, a vertical or horizontal through like hallway, like,

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:03.600
and, you know, we, we joke about that being a problem in today's games, these hallway games,

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:06.500
but this, this game does suffer from that same problem.

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:11.220
There's, there's very little, uh, platforming outside of just, you know, oh, well, if I

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:13.720
start at the bottom of this level, I have to move up to the top.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:16.060
Or if I started on the left side, I got to get to the right.

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:17.720
I don't know.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:21.800
I thought that there were, I definitely found plenty of times where I was like a little

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:26.300
lost or trying to, trying to retrace my steps to figure out how I got here, especially

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:29.540
later when you start backtracking, which I know was something I think

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:34.000
we, we debated about during Metroid, whether backtracking is, is okay, or if it's kind

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:38.820
of annoying. And I, and I don't even remember where I landed in terms of Metroid, but I think in this game,

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:42.300
at least granted, I only got about a little more than halfway through this game.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:46.280
So maybe I would have gotten more annoyed with the backtracking, but, uh, in this game,

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:47.600
I felt like I didn't mind it.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:49.380
It felt like, like an organic way.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:51.800
Like I'm, I'm getting to the next level.

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:54.740
It's not just like, it made travel feel like, I don't know.

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:57.300
I had some agency of like deciding, okay, how do I get here?

00:31:57.400 --> 00:31:59.100
And where do I go next? There's enough branching.

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:03.380
Like, I mean, obviously the branches meet up again most of the time, but there, there

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:07.620
are enough like different ways that you can traverse that like, yeah, maybe the general

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:12.980
direction you're going is to the right, but it's like, there, there are like those very

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:21.000
short halt, like hallway, like, uh, zones that you'll go through a door, hop over two screens

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:21.900
and go through another door.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:26.680
But that's also broken up by much larger environments throughout these levels.

00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:32.700
Sure. I would just argue that I'm not, I'm not certain that anything meaningful happens between those.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:36.300
I mean, I'm just, I'm cheating now and I'm pulling up these maps and I'm looking at them

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:41.820
and it really is kind of like you, you know, these are maps that a three-year-old could solve

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:44.540
with a pen to, you know, to find the exits between them.

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:49.080
It's not like there's these diverging paths where like, oh, well, you know, you got to

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:52.440
go this way first because there's a thing over here and then you go down because you

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:55.620
couldn't have gone straight there first because you don't have this yet.

00:32:55.620 --> 00:33:00.300
You know? I guess I just don't know what it's harming.

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:04.300
Okay. Yeah. It's maybe they're simple when you're zoomed out.

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:12.400
Um, but if it's, if the environment is conducive to the gameplay experience that, uh, that

00:33:12.400 --> 00:33:16.720
the game is, um, is supplying to you, like, is it, is this bad?

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:23.540
Like, is it, um, I guess I'm not sure what the actual, like, what the harm is here.

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.020
Well, it's a game about exploration, right?

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:27.920
What? It's a game about exploration.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:32.820
Like you're trying to explore this brand new underground world that Jason's just found.

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:37.500
And it's just, it's a little empty and you find these power-ups and yeah, there's, there's

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:42.400
backtracking, but it's nothing, you know, to get back to the point of like exploration,

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:46.860
there's not a lot to actually explore rather than just moving forward.

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:54.120
I don't know. Maybe I just was much worse at navigating, but I felt like even if it is like, there's

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:58.440
not that many other ways you can go, I did feel like I, you know, I was having a lot

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:04.060
of agency. If you know, it was probably an illusion, but it felt like I had agency in, in deciding

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:06.160
where to go next. And like, should I go this way or that way?

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:09.720
And like you said, somebody said, like, maybe you'd end up just in the same place no matter

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:14.000
what, but there were like sometimes where this was something I didn't care for at first,

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:17.880
but there were some times where you'd, you'd find a top down section where you're like,

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:19.000
oh, this is where I'm supposed to go next.

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:22.680
And it turns out there's just a bunch of like power-ups and pickups in there.

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:27.020
So I don't know, maybe again, I'd have to play more of it.

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:30.600
And then I'd be thinking like, well, yeah, this is getting like a little too linear, but

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:34.240
I never felt like the game was saying like, you have to, I don't know.

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:37.580
I never felt like it was like, I have to really like the game was about searching and finding.

00:34:37.640 --> 00:34:43.340
It just felt like it was about navigating these, these different levels and, you know,

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:47.480
exploration in a story sense, but it didn't feel like I needed to be like, I never thought

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:49.100
that the game was trying to put me in a maze.

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:52.120
It didn't feel like hallways to me.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:55.020
It didn't feel like Final Fantasy 10 hallways.

00:34:55.160 --> 00:34:56.700
It felt like there was more to it.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:00.160
But I mean, zooming out, I'm looking at it as well.

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:01.280
Yeah, they're simple.

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:04.520
But when I was playing the game, it didn't feel that way.

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:10.920
Totally. And again, I keep saying I didn't get further than level four or five, but, but, you know,

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:15.040
there was the one level when you get the hover ability and you have to kind of go back to

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:20.340
level one and you got to sort of remember like, wait, there were platforms that I couldn't

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:21.460
reach at the beginning of the game.

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.140
I bet I can get back there and find those.

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:29.400
To me, it just felt more about like unlocking the new paths and, you know, remembering where

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.880
to go next and trying to figure, you know, that kind of thing out.

00:35:31.880 --> 00:36:01.860
But I think that kind of thing.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:03.820
Thank you.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:35.740
Thank you.

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:02.760
So that's exploration.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:07.400
But in terms of just progression as you're getting these upgrades and you're going along

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:11.800
in the game, I mean, we always love good power-ups in our games.

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:14.020
And I think we briefly touched upon this already.

00:37:14.140 --> 00:37:18.140
But I think that the power-ups in this game for Sophia the Third and also for your blaster

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:20.280
are really meaningful.

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:23.960
And you feel powerful assuming you collect everything.

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:28.160
I don't think you actually have to collect everything to get to Area 8.

00:37:29.060 --> 00:37:38.940
Yeah, I mean, I agree on the uniqueness of the power-ups that you get and the feeling

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:41.920
of power that you have when you're using them.

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:49.420
I guess I don't know if you need everything because I don't think that I've explored the

00:37:49.420 --> 00:37:52.340
entire game and I may have missed things.

00:37:52.700 --> 00:37:56.500
But yeah, I mean, that's sort of what I...

00:37:56.500 --> 00:38:03.020
I guess I very much want some complexity to level design.

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:11.560
But again, when the core experience is using these upgrades and they did that well, I guess

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:14.440
that's where I see that differently.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:20.500
And I can confirm now that you actually do need all of the Sophia upgrades in order to

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:21.620
get to the end. Gotcha.

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:23.000
It was just...

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:23.840
I guess I was...

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:25.600
I didn't even know about...

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.900
Because I didn't get past Area 5 either, Joe.

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:32.720
I didn't know about the key that gets you to Area 5.

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:35.400
That's what your frog drops, right?

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.680
I never saw it. So I can only debate.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:43.680
Okay. But, you know, I think that these power-ups are really cool.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:48.660
And the fact that it's not just like, you know, the hyper beam and the crusher beam.

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:51.280
If that was like the whole game of just...

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:52.540
And even those are cool for the record.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:57.040
I'm just... You know, the fact that you can destroy blocks that you've seen before, but now without that

00:38:57.040 --> 00:38:57.840
upgrade, you couldn't do it.

00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:59.920
That's also very Metroid-like.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:05.680
I just appreciate that they actually changed maneuverability of Sophia as well.

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:07.800
The hover one working really well.

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:12.560
And, you know, the dive engine being something that...

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:17.600
I just assumed that, like, oh, because of Area 1, water's always going to suck in this

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:23.140
game with Sophia. And it's nice that they actually, like, do that to reward you later.

00:39:23.420 --> 00:39:24.600
You know, much later, apparently.

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:30.300
I wanted to talk about the water engine because I feel like it's this bittersweet thing in

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:35.040
game design that I like in theory when I'm thinking about it.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.160
And sometimes when I'm playing it.

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:42.180
But where the game almost, like, goes out of its way to put a whole level of just really

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.080
frustrating gameplay in there.

00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:48.200
I don't know how you guys felt, but I felt like that whole underwater part was very, very

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:50.280
frustrating. But then the...

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:54.620
Getting the power-up that suddenly makes that easier is, like, the most satisfying thing

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:57.820
in the world because you've spent so much time being frustrated.

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:01.680
So it is, like, this kind of K-22 where it's like, well, you have to frustrate your player

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.660
before you can, like, give them a reward this great.

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:09.560
And I think that it's actually maybe, like, a little bit of a less common thing now than,

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:13.140
like, when we were kids playing, like, really any...

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:14.820
I'm trying to even think of a good example.

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:16.200
Like, you know, any of these...

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:17.040
I mean, even, like...

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:18.160
Nah, I can't think of a good example.

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:21.760
But, like, any of these, like, older games that we played that were, like, difficult...

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:25.440
And Final Fantasy is a great example where, like, you get the airship and suddenly, like,

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:26.940
traversal... You have to trudge through...

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.000
Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:31.980
And... But that wouldn't be as significant if you started out with the airship.

00:40:32.060 --> 00:40:34.780
You just... You would just not have the appreciation for the size of the world.

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:37.100
And I think it's kind of a similar thing here, too.

00:40:37.340 --> 00:40:40.860
You really appreciate that power-up you got because, really, you're only going to use

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:42.320
it a couple more times when you're in the water.

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:45.840
But you have the context of how hard it was to get through the water and you're just

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:49.360
like, thank God my life has gotten so much easier now because I...

00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:51.180
Because I earned it, you know?

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:52.740
Yeah. I agree.

00:40:52.920 --> 00:40:55.300
Saying it right now, I don't even have anything bad to say about it.

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:58.180
But sometimes when I'm playing, I'm like, just give me the freaking thing.

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:03.720
It's the fact that, you know, Joe, that each of these power-ups does something meaningful

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.860
to the environment as well, you know?

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:09.960
So I think besides just the fact that they, you know, the hover might make it easier for

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:16.200
you to dodge enemies, it's also, like, required movement to get around things in the same

00:41:16.200 --> 00:41:19.900
sense that, like, that hyper beam that you get early on, like, that's the only way to

00:41:19.900 --> 00:41:24.460
destroy the Guardian that is blocking area, too, you know?

00:41:24.860 --> 00:41:31.020
Yeah. Things like that are cool because they have, like, this two-fold thing of you can see the

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:36.800
reason why you can't progress further, but you need the power-up in order to do that.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:41.080
And I think that goes back to that exploration thing I was talking about earlier of, I was

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:45.260
just really posing the question of, does this game need more exploration?

00:41:45.260 --> 00:41:49.700
Because a game like Metroid, where you would just keep, if you just kept moving forward and

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:54.220
getting the power-up that you needed and then immediately moving on, wouldn't feel as good

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:56.300
of, like, a lost-in-space kind of journey.

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:57.540
And this is no different.

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.360
I mean, in the English one, we're not in space.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.480
But in the Japanese version of the game, you're in space.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:04.580
And so you have this feeling of exploration.

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:06.580
And I just wondered if they could have pushed that a little further.

00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:08.220
Yeah, I think they could have.

00:42:08.300 --> 00:42:10.560
But I just think it would have been a totally different creative decision.

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:11.840
You know, like, it would have been a different...

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:13.960
Yeah, to me, it just didn't feel like that's what they were going for.

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:16.120
Like, that lost-in-space.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.460
And it was more just, like, go through this adventure.

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:20.660
And, you know, there's a little bit of find-your-way.

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:21.840
But it never felt like it was...

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:25.560
Like, Metroid, I remember it feeling like, oh, they want you to feel scared and lost.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:26.480
And this is almost horror.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:29.220
Where this was like, ah, you're just going and you're shooting...

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:31.840
You know, you're shooting these mutants and you're trying to get to the bottom of this.

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:33.300
And this felt a little more light-hearted to me.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:39.320
There's another thing, too, with a lack of a map and having to complete this game in one sitting.

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:41.820
There's no save feature like there was in Metroid.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:43.380
Does that bother you guys?

00:42:43.540 --> 00:42:47.300
The map thing actually doesn't bother me here because of the straightforwardness of it.

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:51.480
But the completing in one sitting feels like a big ask for Master Blaster.

00:42:51.580 --> 00:42:53.020
No, that is a big problem.

00:42:53.320 --> 00:42:56.620
It needed either passwords or a save function.

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:04.640
I mean, I also don't mind there not being a map because, yeah, they all have like a...

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:07.780
There's sameness to it in some regards.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:09.400
But there's still...

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:11.920
Like, these maps are memorizable.

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:17.220
Like, there's still landmark-ish things that you can, like, put in a mental map.

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:20.820
Or if you truly needed to, you could just draw your own map.

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:24.760
You know, I think, like, for games that are this basic, that's actually a little fun.

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:28.240
It wouldn't be fun in a 3D game to draw a map.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:30.180
But in these kinds of games, it is fun.

00:43:30.860 --> 00:43:36.060
Yeah. And not to mention that I do think the maps that are provided in the manual, while not super detailed...

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:38.360
When I first looked at them, I was like, that's ridiculous.

00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:39.440
Like, how is that even helpful?

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:41.940
But then throughout the game, it actually was pretty helpful.

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:43.620
Like, especially because, like...

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:51.400
I think if they hadn't included those maps in the manual, I would have been, like, a little more frustrated with the fact that, like, the door to...

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:55.220
I don't remember exactly, but, like, the door to stage 7 is in stage 1.

00:43:55.400 --> 00:44:00.080
But then the door... Like, it's not chronological, and there's really no way in the game of having any idea.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:05.700
So it was nice to be able to check, okay, at least I know I need to get back to stage 3 in order to move on, or whatever it was.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:08.700
So that I was okay with.

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:11.860
But to your first question, like, yeah, that's...

00:44:11.860 --> 00:44:16.640
I hate the idea of, like, that they made this so you have to go through in one sitting.

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:20.320
That's one thing that I think is just, like, the...

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:23.660
It was just not, like, the norm at the time.

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:33.520
And, like, I think that that and, like, the idea of having a set number of lives are just two things that I could completely, almost all the time, no matter what, do without.

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:34.680
And, yeah, they also...

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:35.880
They do give you continues.

00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:42.820
And they start at the beginning of whichever level you are on or whichever, like, area you are in.

00:44:42.820 --> 00:44:46.700
And so it's not quite a wipeout of progress.

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:47.620
But they still...

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:52.300
I think they should have given you unlimited continues if they were going to do it that way.

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:54.820
Because, again, there's no scoring.

00:44:55.060 --> 00:44:57.000
They brag about there not being any scoring.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.200
So why not just give us unlimited continues?

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:12.640
Yeah, and a third thing, just because we're talking about it, and I feel like all these complaints go hand in hand, is what we talked about earlier, how you can actually get stuck and you are forced to kill yourself, to lose a life.

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:17.960
One of your precious lives in this game that only has a set amount of lives that you have to finish in one sitting.

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:22.580
Like, all compounding at each other just, like, really kind of hurt the experience, in my opinion.

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:31.740
You know, you did make me think, though, Joe, that we didn't really talk about the health meter, and I think that's important in a game that wants you to complete it in one sitting.

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:33.880
Did you guys think that was enough health?

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:35.220
I actually found it pretty generous.

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:38.620
This game would suck if you were, like, one or two hits and you were dead.

00:45:39.060 --> 00:45:43.980
I like that. And the health itself is given out pretty freely by the enemies.

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:49.520
Yeah, I agree. It is generous, and refills are also generous.

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:51.040
I think that you're right.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:46:00.720
I wouldn't enjoy this game as much if it didn't have some forgiveness for your very common, I mean, sorry, very frequent mistakes, at least for me.

00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:07.280
Yeah, I agree. I think it was, you know, it wasn't like, it wasn't, they didn't make it too easy, but they definitely made it comfortable compared to a lot of games.

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:14.540
Like, you know, I never felt like I was just going to die on my way to the next stage unless it was really getting difficult.

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:17.400
Like, I never felt like it was cheap or anything.

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:20.380
A little off topic here, but this got me thinking.

00:46:20.380 --> 00:46:34.400
On NES games, do you think you're just more likely to enjoy a game that might be a little easier in the sense that it's not a one-hit kill, you know, does that help your enjoyment of the game?

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:37.580
Or do you think you could like a game where it's one hit and you're out?

00:46:37.580 --> 00:46:39.740
It at least makes it more accessible.

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:42.880
Like, it makes it easier for me to like it.

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:58.820
So, that's why I kind of would prefer a game that has, like, a difficulty curve where, you know, I feel comfortable for a good amount of time so that I can accept the challenge that it plays, that it puts before me later.

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:10.340
So, like, in games that are hard right off the bat, like, aside from, like, more modern games, like, it's easier for me to get into.

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:15.060
But in old games that are hard right off the bat, I just have a hard time getting into them.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:24.140
Yeah, I think I need to be in a very specific mindset where, like, if I sit down with a friend, I'm like, hey, let's try and beat this crazy hard game that, you know, everyone tries to beat.

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:29.080
You know, let's try and beat, I would say Ghosts and Goblins, but that didn't turn out to be quite as hard as we thought it was going to be.

00:47:29.260 --> 00:47:34.620
But, you know, just like any of these quintessential games, you know, the Japanese Super Mario Brothers 2.

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:39.760
Like, let's try and beat that tonight because that's supposed to be really hard and, like, that's supposed to be, like, a big challenge.

00:47:40.040 --> 00:47:49.380
And I know it's not the same thing, but, like, maybe I would like to play, like, a game that's one hit kill if I'm, like, just in a particularly, like, really challenge myself mood.

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:59.700
But I'd say, like, nine times out of ten when I sit down and play a video game, I want to be moderately challenged, but also not to the point where, like, I have to do the same thing a hundred times before I can, like, become skilled.

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:08.440
Like, I need to actually, like, gain a new, like, life skill of playing this game in order to, like, even comfortably play it.

00:48:08.440 --> 00:48:19.920
Yeah, it's kind of like winter games is how I imagine it, where there was that ice skating minigame that, like, it didn't matter what you did, your button inputs did nothing, and we all scored, like, zeros.

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:26.100
And there's just no reason to keep trying that after, like, three times of being like, well, this is just dumb.

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:31.780
Right, yeah, you'd have to, like, really, really want to commit to, like, learning the language of that.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:44.860
But, um, or, or for me, it's almost like, I know it's, this is different, but, like, the analogy being, like, I love Pokemon, and once in a while, I might be like, oh, you know, it'd be really cool to do a Nuzlocke challenge, where when a Pokemon dies, I can't use it ever again.

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:50.600
Like, I would hate if the game was like that, but, like, once in a while, I'm like, oh, that's fun because it's such a hard challenge.

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:54.720
Uh, but for the most part, I like the kind of, like, medium level of difficulty.

00:48:54.720 --> 00:49:06.000
I actually see that a lot in, uh, really good NES players on, on Twitter will post a lot about, like, them adding challenges to NES games they're very good at, where they try to beat, like, Super Mario Bros.

00:49:06.140 --> 00:49:08.120
1 without ever taking damage.

00:49:08.380 --> 00:49:11.320
Oh, okay, so this isn't just, like, take a shot if you take damage.

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:15.900
No, no, no, it's just, like, you, you, you reset the game if you take damage.

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:18.820
Damn. Yeah, and I think that's, I think that's great.

00:49:18.820 --> 00:49:28.500
You know, there's other people who do, like, no mushroom ever, you know, so you can't even, uh, allow yourself to, uh, take a hit because you'll start back at the, uh, at the beginning of the level.

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:30.240
I, I like challenges like that.

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:38.940
I will say that I'm starting to notice, as a personal bias, that I appreciate games like this a lot more than games that are one-hit KOs.

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:43.680
And I don't know if that means that easier games are likely to be received better by me.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:55.840
I wouldn't say that as, like, a, as a, as a be-all, end-all statement, but I appreciate a game that is willing to, uh, accept mistakes than a game that expects you to play perfect.

00:49:56.140 --> 00:50:03.160
I mean, I'll admit that the only reason I've never played Ninja Gaiden is because I never wanted to deal with one-hit kill.

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:11.900
And that was, like, the idea in my head of, that's a one-hit kill game, and, I mean, I'm going to have to play it for this.

00:50:12.020 --> 00:50:14.640
All three of them. Yeah, but, yeah, all three.

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:18.560
Uh, but I, I guess it'll be, it'll be interesting confronting that.

00:50:18.700 --> 00:50:26.480
Yeah, I think, I think, like, for me, it comes down to, like, just the fact that there's, it's hard to quantify because there's really, like, an art to making things that challenging.

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:34.640
I think it's really easy to make things incredibly difficult and not fun, you know, like, just by making things, like, almost unfairly difficult.

00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:37.040
And maybe, like, a lot of NES games might have.

00:50:37.160 --> 00:50:45.880
I think that's, that whole NES hard thing a lot of times is talking about that, where it's, like, it's hard because, because it's not perfect, it's not really well designed.

00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:50.140
Or, like, that, like, that kind of thing is where most of my frustration comes from.

00:50:50.260 --> 00:50:58.580
If something is designed to be hard and it's still designed well and it's satisfying, I've never played a Dark Souls game, but from what I hear, that's, like, the perfect example.

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:01.320
Uh, that, uh, you know, I welcome that.

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:03.280
I just might not always be in the mood for it.

00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:14.600
Exactly. We haven't talked at all about the bosses in this game, and I think that, you know, it's, it's interesting that they're locked to the top-down perspective.

00:51:14.860 --> 00:51:19.420
Were you guys ever expecting, like, a bigger fight with Sophia the Third as your main helm?

00:51:20.660 --> 00:51:29.500
Um, I may have thought that when I started the game, but when they sort of, when I played the first one, I kind of got the idea that this is just how it was going to be.

00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:30.820
Um, yeah.

00:51:31.180 --> 00:51:40.420
And, uh, real quick sidebar, I'm surprised that you didn't recognize, uh, the, uh, the creature on the final.

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:49.220
It's so funny that you say that because I was, while, uh, while you guys were going on your rant earlier, I was watching the ending of the game and I was like, wow, he is the final boss.

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:50.840
So you do get to see him that way.

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:56.340
Yeah. Um, and also in the manual, they say, we can't show you the final boss.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:59.400
Um, but then it's just right on the cover of the game.

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:05.220
Anyway. No, but, but the, the, that, that guy transforms into, like, something else that they're talking about.

00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:09.820
I see. It transforms into, like, Lord Zed from Power Rangers.

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:16.520
It's pretty cool. Um, but anyway, regarding the bosses, I thought that they were fantastic to look at.

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:24.900
Um, and there, there was enough variety in their behavior that I enjoyed, at least fighting the ones that I did fight.

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:32.500
Um, uh, and it's kind of weird that you kind of fight them on a black background, but I imagine that's for technical reasons.

00:52:32.680 --> 00:52:35.360
I think that has to do with the fact of how good they look.

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:37.740
They probably dedicated all the graphics to the boss.

00:52:37.840 --> 00:52:41.060
These are definitely the best looking bosses I've ever seen in an NES game.

00:52:41.060 --> 00:52:44.160
Yeah. So I, I, I found them very enjoyable.

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:45.320
Yeah, I did, I did too.

00:52:45.460 --> 00:52:50.620
And as far as fighting bosses as Sophia the third, I don't think that really crossed my mind.

00:52:50.620 --> 00:52:59.940
And just because I think I'm, like, conditioned to just think that, like, there's a format to these, you know, that would be like in Legend of Zelda, if all of a sudden there was a boss, like, outside of a dungeon.

00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:02.560
Like, just on the outside map.

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:04.420
Ganon just settles things outside.

00:53:04.700 --> 00:53:06.780
Yeah. He just, like, came looking for you.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:16.100
Well, I mean, in, in the, uh, history of video games, there are many examples of, uh, like, uh, non, non-standard bosses.

00:53:16.100 --> 00:53:21.980
Like, you know, in modern, in modern games, there's, like, quick time event bosses and all that kind of stuff.

00:53:22.100 --> 00:53:33.120
So, and I imagine in retro games that I can't just think of off the top of my head, there are, uh, bosses that you fight in modes of play that it hasn't been established.

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:44.060
But I guess it's safe to assume that if the bosses you fought, the first, if the first boss you fight is in this kind of way, then that's just how you're going to keep fighting them.

00:53:44.060 --> 00:53:52.340
Sure. And I would argue that the, the Legend of Zelda example is actually, uh, a little different because that, that overworld is still the same type of layout.

00:53:52.460 --> 00:53:54.580
Like, you fight as Link the same way that you do.

00:53:54.760 --> 00:54:11.400
Here, it was just more like, I would have, you know, imagined seeing you have, being a fully powered up Sophia the Third and having to use your different beams and the hover and the wall mechanics for, like, tackling a, a giant, uh, cave-like creature.

00:54:11.560 --> 00:54:12.840
That would have been cool. Yeah.

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:15.680
You know, I, and I'm not arguing that I think that that would have been a bad idea.

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:19.640
In fact, when you put it like that, you've, you've pretty much sold me on, on, that would be a pretty cool idea.

00:54:20.200 --> 00:54:29.120
Uh, I just think that the, the, the, so far the language of these NES games has not conditioned me to even have, like, considered that once I was just like, oh, this is the way these games are.

00:54:29.240 --> 00:54:32.380
Like, the bosses are in these worlds, the traversals in this part.

00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:36.700
And yeah, it would have been interesting though to see, especially because it's side-scrolling versus top-down.

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:38.260
Would have been fun.

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:39.700
I lament the loss.

00:54:40.160 --> 00:54:41.020
I'm sure you do.

00:54:41.640 --> 00:55:40.760
But. I have

00:55:40.760 --> 00:55:47.640
I have some quotes here from a different Iwata, not the Iwata of Nintendo fame.

00:55:47.920 --> 00:55:49.560
Yoshiaki Iwata, let's go with that.

00:55:49.800 --> 00:55:54.460
He was the art designer for Blaster Master, and I pulled two quotes from him.

00:55:54.580 --> 00:55:58.680
I know, Sean, you're always loving to hear what these game designers have to say about their games.

00:55:58.880 --> 00:56:00.480
I do. So here we go.

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:05.560
The goal was really to try and pull off the best graphics on the NES to date.

00:56:05.880 --> 00:56:09.780
Simple graphics were more or less the standard on the NES at the time,

00:56:09.780 --> 00:56:14.420
but I had this firm belief that it was possible to do something better, something prettier.

00:56:14.660 --> 00:56:19.400
I feel like we pulled it off, and we were able to show people what could be done on the NES.

00:56:19.880 --> 00:56:23.260
It left an impression around the office, and from what I've heard,

00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:28.600
the visuals influenced the work of other games that were later made by other NES developers as well.

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:33.120
We also didn't want to make a game where the player just proceeds forward.

00:56:33.120 --> 00:56:38.660
We wanted to make the player experience the feeling of excitement that comes from discovering something

00:56:38.660 --> 00:56:45.420
after endeavoring through a difficult search, which is why we composed a map that allows players to move freely between different areas.

00:56:45.720 --> 00:56:49.120
We really put a great deal of thought into that element of the game design,

00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:53.640
and I mean this in the best way possible, but we wanted the player to have a struggle.

00:56:54.140 --> 00:56:55.260
To have a struggle.

00:56:55.400 --> 00:56:56.600
They mean that in the best way possible.

00:56:56.980 --> 00:56:57.900
Oh, yeah, exactly.

00:56:57.900 --> 00:56:59.520
No hard feelings.

00:56:59.860 --> 00:57:02.720
I mean he was definitely right on the graphic side, right?

00:57:02.780 --> 00:57:04.520
As the art designer, he did a great job.

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:13.000
Yeah, I'm hopeful that he was telling the truth about how much influence it's had on more NES games,

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:16.940
because again, this is the best looking NES game so far,

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:20.560
and I'm looking forward to more of those.

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:22.260
It's a beautiful art style.

00:57:22.260 --> 00:57:29.740
Yeah, I can't imagine any licensed games like Back to the Future or Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles looking that good,

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:34.120
but I could see other games even from Sunsoft, who made this one.

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:38.840
I could see them, if they're releasing other games, using this information to their benefit,

00:57:38.840 --> 00:57:41.020
and bringing out more pretty games.

00:57:41.300 --> 00:57:42.760
Oh, he does look like Lord Zed.

00:57:43.460 --> 00:57:45.800
Right? I thought that was good.

00:57:46.340 --> 00:57:48.600
And then, you know, I don't know if you guys know this,

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:53.440
but Blaster Master has a lot of sequels and spinoffs.

00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:57.080
I've recently learned of many of them, yes.

00:57:57.380 --> 00:57:58.640
Okay, okay, that's good.

00:57:58.640 --> 00:57:59.660
You cheated this time.

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:00.880
Yeah, I cheated this time.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.840
Will you keep your reactions as genuine?

00:58:03.320 --> 00:58:05.220
I'll try. Okay, that's good.

00:58:05.380 --> 00:58:07.640
Joe, did you know that Blaster Master is like a franchise?

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:09.120
You know, it's funny.

00:58:09.240 --> 00:58:11.980
I assumed because I had heard of, you know,

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:16.120
Blaster Master seemed to have this, like, B level of, like, popularity.

00:58:16.440 --> 00:58:18.740
I mean, it's not as popular as things like Castlevania and stuff,

00:58:18.800 --> 00:58:19.740
but I had definitely heard of it.

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:21.480
Yeah, it's like a cult classic. This is based on just my perception.

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:23.420
Maybe I'm wrong. Is it a cult classic?

00:58:23.540 --> 00:58:26.880
I would say so. I think a lot of NES fans know about this game

00:58:26.880 --> 00:58:29.780
and would be shocked to hear people, like, discovering it.

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:31.600
Like, what do you mean you never played Blaster Master?

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:35.680
That's actually why I was surprised that I, like I said, I've heard of it,

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:37.640
but I was surprised that I never have seen gameplay

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:39.980
and it's not talked about as much as the other ones

00:58:39.980 --> 00:58:43.500
because it just seems so, I don't know, I don't want to say revolutionary,

00:58:43.500 --> 00:58:45.740
but, like, compared to, like, the NES game so far,

00:58:45.820 --> 00:58:48.280
it just seems like it's, like, quite a step for NES games.

00:58:48.360 --> 00:58:51.500
So, yeah. So, to answer your question, I expected that there were sequels and spinoffs.

00:58:52.000 --> 00:58:52.800
Great. Okay.

00:58:53.080 --> 00:58:57.660
And originally there was no sequel planned for Blaster Master

00:58:57.660 --> 00:59:00.440
because the game sold really poorly,

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:04.960
but that changed when the North American release of Blaster Master came

00:59:04.960 --> 00:59:06.260
and sold really well,

00:59:06.440 --> 00:59:08.540
which is surprising that it didn't do as well in Japan,

00:59:09.160 --> 00:59:12.940
but we wouldn't see another Blaster Master game on the NES.

00:59:13.200 --> 00:59:18.880
It was actually Blaster Master 2 came to Sega Genesis in 1993

00:59:18.880 --> 00:59:20.760
only for North America,

00:59:20.760 --> 00:59:22.380
so there was no, um,

00:59:23.380 --> 00:59:24.720
what was it called? Mega Drive?

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:26.380
I think it was called Mega Drive in Japan.

00:59:26.380 --> 00:59:29.320
Uh, there was no, uh, Blaster Master 2 of Mega Drive.

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:32.260
The difference is that there are eight levels to play through,

00:59:32.540 --> 00:59:36.720
and each level, uh, contains a power-up or a new obtainable weapon

00:59:36.720 --> 00:59:40.340
in which Jason or Sophia can use throughout the game.

00:59:40.640 --> 00:59:41.780
So... I think I was going to say, or Fred.

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:43.260
Right, that would be interesting.

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:44.200
You finally get...

00:59:44.200 --> 00:59:46.260
This time you're Fred searching for Jason.

00:59:46.500 --> 00:59:48.160
Yeah, he jumps down a hole.

00:59:48.340 --> 00:59:50.180
Jason jumps down a hole and Fred's going to find him.

00:59:50.260 --> 00:59:51.380
Yeah, and if you get a chance,

00:59:51.380 --> 00:59:53.900
you should check out the, the look of this one

00:59:53.900 --> 00:59:55.860
because I feel like, for some reason,

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:57.800
the graphics aren't really, like,

00:59:57.880 --> 01:00:00.700
that much more stepped up for the Genesis version,

01:00:00.700 --> 01:00:02.160
and they're actually a little ugly.

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:03.680
Uh, maybe it's just, like, the palette,

01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:06.600
but I wasn't a, I wasn't a fan of the way this one looked.

01:00:07.120 --> 01:00:09.760
And, and then we have Blaster Master Jr.

01:00:10.100 --> 01:00:11.680
or Blaster Master Boy,

01:00:11.820 --> 01:00:13.280
depending on which region you're in,

01:00:13.780 --> 01:00:15.120
and that came out...

01:00:15.120 --> 01:00:15.960
I like Blaster Master Boy.

01:00:16.120 --> 01:00:18.440
Yeah, I like Blaster Master Boy 2

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:20.920
because it's the, if you look at the box arts for the two,

01:00:20.920 --> 01:00:22.680
Boy is spaced out really nicely.

01:00:24.000 --> 01:00:26.620
And that came out on Game Boy in 1992,

01:00:27.460 --> 01:00:29.560
and that's part of that whole, like, weird trend

01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:32.860
of, you know, like, NES, successful NES games

01:00:32.860 --> 01:00:35.460
getting Game Boy ports that have nothing to do

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:37.000
with the original game.

01:00:37.260 --> 01:00:39.100
And this time there's no Sophia,

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:41.260
so it's really just, like, Bomberman.

01:00:41.420 --> 01:00:42.300
Oof. Yeah.

01:00:42.500 --> 01:00:43.660
I don't like that.

01:00:43.840 --> 01:00:44.780
I don't like that either.

01:00:44.780 --> 01:00:47.020
I don't know why you would get rid of Sophia the Third,

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:48.240
but they did,

01:00:48.380 --> 01:00:50.320
and nobody ever played that game.

01:00:50.320 --> 01:00:51.340
That's a fact.

01:00:52.540 --> 01:00:54.500
Blaster Master Enemy Below

01:00:54.500 --> 01:00:56.380
on Game Boy Color in 2000.

01:00:56.860 --> 01:00:59.720
It pretty much is just like the NES game,

01:00:59.720 --> 01:01:00.680
just a whole new game,

01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:03.020
but very little has changed from the NES one.

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:04.020
That's the one that just looks like

01:01:04.020 --> 01:01:05.260
it's super zoomed in.

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:07.120
Yes, because of the...

01:01:07.120 --> 01:01:09.280
I don't really understand graphics

01:01:09.280 --> 01:01:10.680
on how that works with, like,

01:01:10.740 --> 01:01:11.840
the Game Boy and the Game Boy Color,

01:01:11.840 --> 01:01:14.260
but it just has to do with the limitation

01:01:14.260 --> 01:01:16.340
of how many sprites you can have on screen

01:01:16.340 --> 01:01:20.460
and stuff like that. It's the same problem with Metroid 2 Samus Returns

01:01:20.460 --> 01:01:21.920
on the Game Boy. That one is like,

01:01:22.040 --> 01:01:23.460
the whole screen is just Samus.

01:01:25.980 --> 01:01:27.760
And then there's Blaster Master

01:01:27.760 --> 01:01:30.120
blasting again on the PlayStation.

